<article>

## Introduction

Building interactive data visualizations on the web often presents a trade-off between flexibility and performance. When Wikimedia Capacity Building Project (CAPX) needed to display user distribution, language adoption, and skill capacity across global territories, we chose an unconventional approach: a static SVG file paired with React's dynamic styling system. This post explores why we chose this path, the technical challenges we encountered, and the deeper geopolitical considerations embedded in global cartography.

<div class="tech-chips">
  <span class="chip">React</span>
  <span class="chip">SVG</span>
  <span class="chip">TypeScript</span>
  <span class="chip">Wikimedia CAPX</span>
  <span class="chip">ISO 3166-1</span>
</div>

---

## Part I: The Architecture — Why Static SVG?

### The Case for Static SVG

When most developers think of interactive maps, they reach for D3.js or specialized libraries like Mapbox. These tools are powerful and flexible, but they come with significant overhead:

- **Bundle size**: D3 alone is 50+ KB minified
- **Learning curve**: D3's data binding model is sophisticated and context-specific
- **Performance overhead**: Real-time data binding and transitions consume CPU cycles
- **State management complexity**: Integrating D3's state model with React's creates friction

We took a different approach. Instead of generating geographic paths dynamically, we use a pre-rendered blank world map in SVG format sourced from Wikimedia Commons—a static asset that changes rarely, if ever.

### Coupling React Styling with Static SVG

The key insight is this: **SVG elements are just DOM elements**. Once loaded, we can target them with JavaScript, apply CSS, bind event listeners, and manipulate their attributes—all the things D3 does, but with React's declarative model.

```tsx
// Load the SVG from Wikimedia Commons
useEffect(() => {
  fetch('/static/images/BlankMap-World.svg')
    .then((response) => response.text())
    .then((text) => {
      setSvgContent(text);
    });
}, []);

// Apply colors and interactivity based on React state
useEffect(() => {
  if (!svgContainerRef.current || !svgContent) return;

  const container = svgContainerRef.current;
  container.innerHTML = svgContent;

  const svg = container.querySelector('svg');
  const landElements = svg.querySelectorAll('.landxx');

  landElements.forEach((element) => {
    const countryCode = getCountryCodeFromElement(element);
    const territory = countryCode ? getTerritoryByCountry(countryCode) : undefined;
    const color = getTerritoryColor(territory);

    applyTerritoryStylesToElement(element, color, !!territory);
    attachTerritoryEventListeners(element, territory);
  });
}, [svgContent, darkMode, mapOrientation, /* ...dependencies */]);
```

<div class="insight-box">
  <strong>Key advantages of this approach:</strong> No D3 or heavy map libraries required. State updates drive visual changes. Static SVG renders instantly; we only update styles, not redraw. Standard SVG semantics work with screen readers. A pre-generated SVG is smaller than the code to generate it.
</div>

---

## Part II: Technical Challenges

<ul class="challenge-list">
  <li>
    <span class="challenge-badge">Challenge 1</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Parsing Country Codes from SVG Elements</strong><br/>
      The Wikimedia Commons blank map uses ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country codes in element IDs or classes. Our backend uses both alpha-2 and alpha-3 codes — and sometimes refers to countries by Wikimedia territory groupings — requiring careful translation including special cases like Indonesia's code conflicting with the word "id".
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="challenge-badge">Challenge 2</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Color Interpolation Across Multiple View Modes</strong><br/>
      The map supports three view modes: users, languages, and capacities. Each has different color schemes, and the intensity of coloring should reflect data density. We needed a flexible color interpolation system for smooth gradients — blue for users, green for languages, purple for capacities.
    </div>
  </li>
  <li>
    <span class="challenge-badge">Challenge 3</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Responsive SVG Scaling and Touch Interactions</strong><br/>
      SVG viewBox attributes ensure scalability, but we needed careful handling of responsive sizing (280px mobile to 450px desktop), click-based selection for touch devices where hover states don't work, and memory-safe event listener management.
    </div>
  </li>
</ul>

### Country code parsing

```tsx
const getCountryCodeFromElement = (element: Element): string | null => {
  const elementId = element.getAttribute('id') || '';
  const classList = element.getAttribute('class') || '';
  const classes = classList.split(' ');

  // Try ID first (alpha-2 format)
  if (elementId.length === 2 && ISO_ALPHA2_TO_ALPHA3[elementId.toLowerCase()]) {
    return elementId.toLowerCase();
  }

  // Try class names
  for (const cls of classes) {
    const lowerCls = cls.toLowerCase();
    if (lowerCls.length === 2 && lowerCls !== 'id' && ISO_ALPHA2_TO_ALPHA3[lowerCls]) {
      return lowerCls;
    }
  }

  // Special case for Indonesia (commonly labeled 'id')
  if (elementId.toLowerCase() === 'id' || classes.includes('id')) {
    return 'id';
  }

  return null;
};
```

### Color interpolation

```tsx
const interpolateColor = (lightColor: string, baseColor: string, intensity: number) => {
  const r1 = Number.parseInt(lightColor.slice(1, 3), 16);
  const g1 = Number.parseInt(lightColor.slice(3, 5), 16);
  const b1 = Number.parseInt(lightColor.slice(5, 7), 16);
  const r2 = Number.parseInt(baseColor.slice(1, 3), 16);
  const g2 = Number.parseInt(baseColor.slice(3, 5), 16);
  const b2 = Number.parseInt(baseColor.slice(5, 7), 16);

  const r = Math.round(r1 + (r2 - r1) * intensity);
  const g = Math.round(g1 + (g2 - g1) * intensity);
  const b = Math.round(b1 + (b2 - b1) * intensity);

  return {
    r, g, b,
    hex: `#${r.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')}${g.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')}${b.toString(16).padStart(2, '0')}`
  };
};
```

