## Part III: The Geopolitical Dimensions

### Beyond the Map: Territories as Territories

Here's where things get interesting. This isn't just a technical artifact—the map represents **political choices**.

<div class="insight-box">
  <strong>This is geopolitics embedded in code.</strong> When you color a map, you're making political statements about which countries belong together. Wikimedia's organizational territories are not geographic regions in the traditional sense — they reflect linguistic affinities, institutional capacity, historical relationships, and economic development patterns.
</div>

Wikimedia divides the world into 8 territories:

<div class="territory-grid">
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>SSA</strong>Sub-Saharan Africa</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>NWE</strong>Northern & Western Europe</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>ESEAP</strong>East, Southeast Asia, & Pacific</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>LAC</strong>Latin America & Caribbean</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>CEECA</strong>Central & Eastern Europe & Central Asia</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>SA</strong>South Asia</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>MENA</strong>Middle East & North Africa</div>
  <div class="territory-card"><strong>NA</strong>North America</div>
</div>

### The North-Up / South-Up Toggle: Cartographic Bias

The most controversial feature in our map is the orientation toggle. Why does this matter?

**The Mercator projection's legacy**: Most world maps show north at the top and use the Mercator projection, which:
- Exaggerates the size of northern landmasses (Greenland appears larger than Africa)
- Reinforces a "North as superior" visual hierarchy
- Is a colonial-era choice designed for European maritime navigation

**The South-Up perspective** (rotating the map 180°) is increasingly used by educators and organizations like Google to challenge the assumption that north = up/superior, provide a Southern-hemisphere perspective, and question inherited cartographic conventions.

```tsx
svg.style.transform = mapOrientation === 'south-up' ? 'rotate(180deg)' : 'none';
```

<div class="insight-box">
  <strong>This simple CSS transformation represents a profound shift in perspective.</strong> It's not just about visual novelty — it's about acknowledging that maps are not neutral. They encode power relationships.
</div>

### Territories and Marginalization

Consider these territorial assignments:
- **Island nations in the Pacific** are grouped with East Asia, not with their nearest geographic neighbors
- **Australia** is with Asia, not with the Americas despite similar development levels
- **North Africa** is grouped with the Middle East, not Sub-Saharan Africa, reflecting political/linguistic divisions

Each choice reflects organizational priorities, historical alliances, and implicit assumptions about where cultural/institutional affinities lie. The map becomes a record of how an organization chooses to partition the world.

---

## Part IV: Building with Territories in Code

The mapping between API territories and display territories requires careful consideration:

```tsx
// Build mapping from API territory to Wikimedia territory using either dynamic names or fallback
export function buildApiTerritoryToWikimediaMap(
  territories: Record<string, string> | null | undefined
): Record<string, string> {
  const map: Record<string, string> = {};

  if (territories && Object.keys(territories).length > 0) {
    const NORMALIZED_TERRITORY_MAP: Record<string, string> = {
      subsaharanafrica: 'SSA',
      northernandwesterneurope: 'NWE',
      eastsoutheastasiaandpacific: 'ESEAP',
      // ... etc
    };

    Object.entries(territories).forEach(([id, name]) => {
      const normalizedName = name
        .toLowerCase()
        .replace(/&/g, 'and')
        .replace(/[^a-z0-9]/g, '')
        .trim();
      const wikimediaId = NORMALIZED_TERRITORY_MAP[normalizedName];
      if (wikimediaId) {
        map[id] = wikimediaId;
      }
    });

    if (Object.keys(map).length > 0) {
      return map;
    }
  }

  // Fallback to hardcoded mapping
  return { ...API_TERRITORY_ID_TO_WIKIMEDIA };
}
```

This function handles the reality that backends may have their own territory nomenclature, requiring translation to the display layer's Wikimedia territories.

---

## Part V: Dark Mode and Accessibility

Color is not just aesthetic—it's functional and accessible:

```tsx
const getBaseAndLightColors = (currentViewMode: ViewMode, isDarkMode: boolean) => {
  const light = isDarkMode ? '#1A2F3A' : '#E8F4FC';

  switch (currentViewMode) {
    case 'languages':
      return {
        baseColor: isDarkMode ? '#10B85C' : '#0B8E46',
        lightColor: light,
      };
    // ... other modes
  }
};
```

- **Dark mode** uses higher-contrast colors and reduced brightness for background elements
- **Color gradients** use carefully chosen pairs for CVD (color vision deficiency) accessibility
- **Fallback colors** ensure unmapped countries remain visible but distinguished

---

## Part VI: Lessons and Trade-offs

<div class="pros-cons">
  <div class="pros-cons-card pros">
    <h4>What Worked Well</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>No complex library abstractions — just JavaScript on SVG</li>
      <li>Static SVG renders instantly; only color updates require repaints</li>
      <li>Easy to add new interaction modes (hover, click, selection)</li>
      <li>Fits naturally into React component lifecycle</li>
      <li>SVG file is independent; updates don't break the code</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
  <div class="pros-cons-card cons">
    <h4>Trade-offs & Limitations</h4>
    <ul>
      <li>Can't easily reproject or transform for different purposes</li>
      <li>No animated paths or geodesic calculations without D3</li>
      <li>Large countries dominate visually on a flat map</li>
      <li>No zoom/pan without more complex SVG manipulation</li>
      <li>Data encoded through color alone; no size or opacity encoding</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</div>

---

## Conclusion: Maps as Political Documents

Building this world map forced us to confront uncomfortable truths: **Every map is a choice.** By choosing static SVG, Wikimedia territories, the north-up/south-up toggle, and specific color schemes, we're making political and cultural statements.

The technical decision to use a static SVG was practical—it's fast, simple, and effective. But the deeper lesson is that visualization is never neutral. The territories you display, the colors you choose, the perspective you default to—these all reflect values, priorities, and power relationships.

<div class="insight-box">
  The next time you see a world map in an application, ask yourself:<br/>
  <strong>Which territories are grouped together, and why? What perspective is the default view? Who benefits from this particular representation? What's invisible or deemphasized?</strong><br/><br/>
  That's the story your map is telling. Make sure it's the story you intend to tell.
</div>

